1 DIRECTORY

   Lists files on a mounted volume, laid out as VMS's DIRECTORY lays
   them out: a heading for each directory, the files' names, and the
   totals.

   Format

     DIRECTORY [filespec[,...]]

2 Parameter

 filespec[,...]

   The files to list.  Wildcards are allowed, and a list of
   specifications is listed in turn, with one grand total.  A part left
   out of the first is the default device and directory's (see SET
   DEFAULT), and a part left out of a later one is the one before it's;
   but a version left out is every version.  With no filespec, DIRECTORY
   lists *.*;*, every version of every file in the default directory.

2 Description

   Each directory's files are listed under the heading "Directory
   DUA0:[NAME]", four names to a line, or one to a line with the columns
   the qualifiers ask for, then "Total of n files." after the directory,
   and "Grand total of ..." after more than one.  A specification that
   finds no file is %DIRECT-W-NOFILES.

2 Qualifiers
/DATE

   Shows each file's creation date.

/FILE

   Shows each file's file ID.

/FULL

   Shows everything the file's header says: its file ID, the blocks used
   and allocated, owner, dates, organization, record format and
   attributes, protection, and access control list.

/HEADING

      /[NO]HEADING

   /NOHEADING leaves out the directory headings, and shows each file by
   its whole name, one to a line.

/OUTPUT

      /OUTPUT[=filespec]

   Writes the listing to a file instead of the terminal: filespec (type
   .LIS unless it gives one), or DIRECTORY.LIS.  %DIRECT-W-NOFILES still
   goes to the terminal.

/OWNER

   Shows each file's owner, as [SYSTEM] or [group,member].

/PROTECTION

   Shows each file's protection: the access the system, the owner, the
   group, and the world have, in that order, as (RWED,RWED,RE,).

/SIZE

      /SIZE[=keyword]

   Shows each file's size in blocks: USED (the default), ALLOCATION, or
   ALL (used/allocated).

/TOTAL

      /TOTAL

   Shows only each directory's heading and its total of files.

/TRAILING

      /[NO]TRAILING

   /NOTRAILING leaves out the totals.

/VERSIONS

      /VERSIONS=n

   Lists only each file's n newest versions: /VERSIONS=1 shows the
   newest alone.  The value must be at least 1.

2 Examples

   1.$ DIRECTORY

     Lists every file in the default directory.

   2.$ DIRECTORY/VERSIONS=2 *.TXT

     Lists the two newest versions of each .TXT file.

   3.$ DIRECTORY/SIZE=ALL/DATE DUA1:[WORK...]

     Lists the files in [WORK] and below it on DUA1, with their sizes
     and creation dates.

   4.$ DIRECTORY/OUTPUT=FILES [A...],[B...]

     Writes the listing of two trees to FILES.LIS.
